<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maryland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maryland is a state of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>. Founded in 1634 as an English proprietary colony of the Calvert family, Lords Baltimore, it offered a refuge for Catholics and grew rich on tobacco worked by indentured servants and enslaved Africans. It was one of the thirteen colonies that declared independence in 1776, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> was carved partly from its land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maryland.jpg" alt="Maryland" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Wheeler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Maryland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1862 New Orleans fell on 29 April, and the two invasions of Kentucky and <b>Maryland</b> ended at Antietam, which Allan Nevins calls the strategic high-water mark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas commerce grew rapidly under the Navigation Acts, which reserved colonial trade for British ships: sugar from Jamaica and Barbados, tobacco from Virginia and <b>Maryland</b> and textiles from India were re-exported across Europe, and Glasgow prospered on tobacco after the union.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1611 and 1614 Adriaen Block, Hendrick Christiaensen and Cornelius Jacobsen May charted the coast from <b>Maryland</b> to Massachusetts, and Block's map was the first to carry the name New Netherland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States Frederick Douglass, who escaped from slavery in <b>Maryland</b> in 1838, became one of the movement’s most famous orators and founded his own newspaper; Harriet Tubman, who escaped in 1849, went back some thirteen times to lead about seventy people to freedom; Sojourner Truth preached…</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In McCulloch v. <b>Maryland</b> in 1819 the Supreme Court read the federal government’s powers broadly, and in 1832 South Carolina claimed the right to nullify a federal tariff, backing down when Congress lowered the tariff and President Andrew Jackson threatened force.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Sarah Bradford's authorised biography of Harriet Tubman, Scenes in the Life of Harriet Tubman (1869), quotes Tubman as saying she used &quot;Go Down Moses&quot; as one of two code songs to communicate with people escaping slavery from <b>Maryland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li></ul></section>
